### The checks the repository passes

Four commands run over the finished repository before it is reviewed:

| Command | Checks |
| --- | --- |
| `npx tsc --noEmit` | The code type-checks. A repository that does not compile is not reviewed. |
| `npx eslint .` | The code is lint-clean. |
| `npx prettier --check .` | The code is formatted. |
| `npx vitest run --coverage` | Your tests pass, with their count and coverage reported. |

They run on the repository as you leave it. `npm run typecheck`,
`npm run lint`, `npm run format`, and `npm test` run the same four locally.

### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site into
`dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory as the entry point. That directory runs correctly when served as-is
at the root of any static file server, and equally when served from a
sub-path, so every URL the build requests resolves relative to the page rather
than the origin root. The scripts that do this are already in `package.json`.

The build is self-contained. The asset tools on this machine's `PATH` are
development tools for producing the files `specs/assets.md` lists and locates;
the produced files are committed, the build bundles them, and neither `npm ci`
nor `npm run build` invokes a tool.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser.
This is a substantial front-end build: a train of cores walking a fixed
channel, an injector firing into it, matching and chaining, four kinds of
machinery, five levels, seven screens, and a produced set of sprites, effects,
and sounds. Aim for a game a person would enjoy playing, where a shot lands
where it was aimed and a chain reads as it happens.

### Hard requirements

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Write the runtime the game stands on: the frame loop and its delta time, the
  canvas fit, keyboard and pointer input, audio with looping cues, loading the
  produced files, and the debug overlay.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, the canvas fit,
  input, audio with looping cues, asset loading, and the debug overlay from
  it.
{{else}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, writing the game inside its framework
  and taking the frame loop, rendering, collision detection, the canvas fit,
  input actions, audio with looping cues, asset loading, and the debug overlay
  from it.
{{/if}}
- Render real graphics on the canvas, drawn as sprites, shapes, and text
  rather than as an ASCII or text-only rendering.
- Ship the produced sprites, sheets, particle systems, and sounds
  `specs/assets.md` lists, wired in as that file states, and draw everything
  else in code. The build fetches nothing at runtime from outside its own
  `dist/`.
- Run in the browser with no backend. Everything needed to play is
  self-contained, and no API key or credential is needed to build, run, or
  play.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in one value, the one the frame loop advances
  and the debug surface reads.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in `VoluteState`, as `specs/state.md` declares
  it.
{{else}}
- Hold the game's whole state in the framework objects the engine owns: the
  game instance, the open level's game state, and its actors.
{{/if}}
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the
  static production build, and the controls.

## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with other developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## The field

The game is played on a fixed field of `960 x 540` logical units, 16:9.

| Figure | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Origin | `(0, 0)` at the top-left corner |
| `x` | increases to the right |
| `y` | increases downward |
| Angles | degrees, `0` along `+x`, increasing toward `+y` |

An angle therefore turns clockwise on screen as it increases. Every position
and distance in this specification is in logical units, every rate is per
second, and every duration is in seconds.

Fitting the field to the browser window is the runtime's work: the uniform
scale that preserves the aspect ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the
device pixel ratio. The complete field is therefore on screen at every window
size, on load and at any pixel density, and the letterbox bars around it carry
the field's background color. Gameplay operates in logical units, independent
of the rendered scale.

## The charges

A core carries one of five charges: `halide`, `sulfur`, `cobalt`, `garnet`,
and `olivine`. Each charge is drawn in a color that stands apart from the
field, from the channel, and from every other charge, and carries a glyph of
its own, so a player tells the five apart without relying on color. The
palette and the glyphs are yours.
